      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe CLOB contract contains a flawed check intended to catch zero-cost trades, but it uses a bitwise AND operation to determine whether either token amount is zero:\n```solidity\nif (baseTokenAmountReceived != quoteTokenAmountSent &&\n    baseTokenAmountReceived & quoteTokenAmountSent == 0\n) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n```\nThis condition is incorrect and overly aggressive: a & b == 0 returns true even when both a and b are non-zero, as long as they have disjoint binary representations (e.g., a=2^60, b=2^61).\n\nThis causes legitimate trades to revert with a ZeroCostTrade() error. Attackers can leverage this to strategically place orders with values that cause matching attempts to fail, leading to:\n- Denial-of-Service (DoS) on matching logic\n- Inability to take certain valid limit orders\n- Market liveness failures in edge cases\n\nThis is especially dangerous for CLOB designs where tick size or pricing makes disjoint binary values likely (e.g., powers-of-two price increments).\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nReplace the incorrect bitwise check with a logical check:\n```solidity\nif (baseTokenAmountReceived != quoteTokenAmountSent &&\n    (baseTokenAmountReceived == 0 || quoteTokenAmountSent == 0)\n) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n```\nThis ensures only genuinely zero-cost trades are rejected.",
      "proofOfConcept": "Add this test to PoC.t.sol:\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {Side} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n\n    error ZeroCostTrade();\n\n    function testPostLimitOrder_TriggersFalseZeroCostTrade() external {\n        address maker = users[0];\n        address taker = users[1];\n\n        uint256 baseAmount = 0x1000000000000000; // 2^60\n        uint256 price = 2 ether;\n        uint256 quoteAmount = (baseAmount * price) / 1e18; // 2^60 * 2 = 2^61\n\n        // ✅ baseAmount & quoteAmount == 0\n        // ✅ baseAmount > 0\n        // ✅ quoteAmount > 0\n\n        // Maker places SELL order\n        setupTokens(Side.SELL, maker, baseAmount, price, true);\n        vm.prank(maker);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(maker, ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: baseAmount,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: NEVER,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        }));\n\n        // Taker BUY order (same price)\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, taker, baseAmount, price, true);\n\n        vm.expectRevert(ZeroCostTrade.selector);\n        vm.prank(taker);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(taker, ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: baseAmount,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: NEVER,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        }));\n    }\n}\n```\nThen, you can see this result:\n```bash\nRan 1 test for test/c4-poc/PoC.t.sol:PoC\n[PASS] testPostLimitOrder_TriggersFalseZeroCostTrade() (gas: 625054)\nTraces:\n  [724554] PoC::testPostLimitOrder_TriggersFalseZeroCostTrade()\n    ├─ [0] VM::startPrank(0x7E5F4552091A69125d5DfCb